From 58949f4a683767abc7767af4c8f11f8969606ddf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 09:47:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat: add cmd/pipeline-wasm

Exposes parse, lint, when-matching, matrix expansion and stage
resolution to JavaScript, so editors, the docs site and configuration
tools can answer "what will actually run" in a browser using the same
code the server runs. A second implementation of when-matching or DAG
resolution would drift, and a tool that disagrees with the server about
what runs is worse than no tool.

One JSON string in, one JSON string out. Traversing js.Value field by
field is the main performance trap in Go WASM.

Four constraints the implementation exists to satisfy, each found by
running real configs through it:

- Logging is disabled, not redirected. A write to stderr from inside a
  synchronous js.Func deadlocks the module, because syscall.Write goes
  through an async fsCall that cannot complete while the callback is
  still on the stack. Metadata.Environ logs at trace level on the happy
  path, so this is reachable without any error occurring.
- match and stages substitute ${VAR} before parsing, as builder.go does.
  Skipping it makes a when block filtering on ${CI_REPO_DEFAULT_BRANCH}
  match nothing, which reads as a broken config rather than a missing
  step.
- Substitution takes a matrix axis. Without one, image: golang:${VERSION}
  expands to a trailing colon and the config stops being valid YAML.
- stages declares the secrets the config references. The compiler
  resolves from_secret while compiling and fails on an unknown name, so
  otherwise no config using a secret can be graphed in a browser. Names
  suffice; a value cannot change the shape of the graph.

Lint deliberately does not substitute: it takes no metadata, and
expanding against an empty environment would blank values and invent
errors.

Nothing panics across the boundary: a panic escaping into JavaScript
takes down the module, turning one malformed config into a dead editor.

Built with GOOS=js GOARCH=wasm: 20.1 MB raw, 3.30 MB brotli.
